WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
INTRO:
Do you guys remember WeWork? A few years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.920
the company became extremely popular amongst&nbsp;
the startup world for renting out office space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.200
This popularity was accelerated by&nbsp;
Softbank investing $10.65 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.360
into the company in hopes of it going public at&nbsp;
$47 billion. But, all these hopes and dreams were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.040
crushed when it came out that instead of investing&nbsp;
this money effectively, the CEO, Adam Neumann, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.400
burning a significant amount of it living it up.&nbsp;
Everything from smoking weed on private jets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.440
purchasing deca-million mansions left and right.&nbsp;
It seems like Adam was under the impression that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:42.000
he had already made it, but his facade would come&nbsp;
crumbling down when WeWork fired him in late 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.800
This was followed up by investors pulling out as&nbsp;
much money as they could and writing off the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.440
as losses. Softbank, for example, wrote down&nbsp;
$9.2 billion in losses in regards to WeWork.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.760
WeWork did eventually go public, but&nbsp;
their market cap today has come in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:04.560
at a much more modest $3.76 billion which&nbsp;
is over 90% lower than their $47 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.320
valuation. After such a nightmare with WeWork, you&nbsp;
would think that Adam Neumann was done for as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:17.680
reputation was toast. But, somehow, in late 2021,&nbsp;
Adam regained his position as a billionaire. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:25.840
here’s the rise and fall of WeWork and&nbsp;
the unexpected comeback of Adam Neumann.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.600
HUMBLE ORIGINS:
Taking a look back, the story of Adam Neumann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.480
dates back to April 25, 1979, to Israel. Adam&nbsp;
didn’t grow up with private jets and mansions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.000
but his family was pretty well off. His father&nbsp;
was a physician and his mother was working towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.200
becoming a physician herself. This was actually&nbsp;
the reason that the family would move to the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.640
when Adam was 7 years old. His mother needed to&nbsp;
complete her medical residency and what better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:58.080
place to do that than the US. The US wasn’t very&nbsp;
kind to Adam though as he had a very tough time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.600
in school due to his dyslexia. He couldn’t read&nbsp;
or write till third grade, and even then, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.600
tasks were a big struggle for Adam. Fortunately,&nbsp;
after 4 years in the US, Adam’s mother completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.960
her residency and the family would move back to&nbsp;
Israel which gave Adam a sense of familiarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.080
Nonetheless, his academic performance didn’t&nbsp;
improve all that much, so after high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.040
Adam would go ahead and join the Israeli Naval&nbsp;
Academy. He would follow up this training with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.880
five years in the Israeli Navy between 1996&nbsp;
and 2001, and he was honorably discharged as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.520
captain. Unsure of what to do next, Adam would&nbsp;
move to New York City to live with his sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.720
who had built up a career as a model. Around&nbsp;
the same time, Adam would enroll in Zicklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.080
Business School and attempt to secure a business&nbsp;
degree, but this was by no means his focus. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.800
seems like what Adam really wanted to do was&nbsp;
pursue entrepreneurship. In college, he entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.920
an entrepreneurship competition and submitted a&nbsp;
project relating to community living. But, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.840
wasn’t very successful as his idea wouldn’t even&nbsp;
pass the first round of eliminations. With no luck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.760
at business competitions, Adam decided to go ahead&nbsp;
and try his luck at real business, but this wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.520
any better. In fact, it was a lot worse given that&nbsp;
he was losing himself and his investor’s money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.600
First, he started a women’s footwear brand&nbsp;
and when that didn’t pick up any steam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.720
he switched over to selling baby clothing starting&nbsp;
up brands including “Egg Baby” and “Krawlers”. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.360
you would guess, these weren’t very successful&nbsp;
endeavors either and Adam’s expenses far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.800
outpaced his revenue. At one point, he was pulling&nbsp;
in $2 million in revenue but spending $3 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.320
Despite the bleak outlook of his brands, Adam&nbsp;
decided to go all-in on business and dropped out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.360
of college with just 4 credits left. He rented&nbsp;
a small warehouse in Dumbo, Brooklyn which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.960
converted into a small office. The office didn’t&nbsp;
help the financial situation of his business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.600
but I think it helped him feel better about the&nbsp;
business. Unfortunately, though, none of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.400
businesses would ever take off, and Adam would&nbsp;
eventually up closing all of them. While Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.640
wasn’t able to start up a successful business,&nbsp;
one thing he was extremely good at was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.920
connections and raising money. And with his next&nbsp;
venture, he would leverage this skill to the max.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.240
STARTING WEWORK:&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.280
The idea of WeWork actually dates back to&nbsp;
Adam’s days in the small warehouse. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.920
the small nature of his business, he didn’t have&nbsp;
many employees and most of the cubicles within his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.040
warehouse were empty. So, Adam reached out to his&nbsp;
landlord and asked them if he could sublease parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.000
of the warehouse. His landlord would agree and&nbsp;
Adam would begin looking for potential tenants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.120
This actually turned into a full-on endeavor&nbsp;
called GreenDesk in 2008 which Adam ran with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.040
a friend Miguel McKelvey. Adam and Miguel were&nbsp;
both big fans of the shared office space idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.960
Adam says that it reminded him of the&nbsp;
Israeli community that he often missed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.560
Anyway, GreenDesk would grow quite substantially&nbsp;
in the late 2000s, and soon enough Adam was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:05:00.000
renting out over 100 office spaces for $350 to&nbsp;
$2400 per month. Despite this success, in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.120
Adam and Miguel would decide to sell the business&nbsp;
to their landlord and cash out. This didn’t mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.920
that Adam was done with the shared office space&nbsp;
business though. Rather, he just wanted a clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.840
slate to start something much bigger. In 2010,&nbsp;
Adam and Miguel began looking for larger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.320
better locations that he could rent out, and this&nbsp;
is when he would run into a real estate developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.600
named Joel Schreiber. Joel really liked the&nbsp;
office sharing idea and offered to become an angel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.040
investor in the company. He didn’t just offer a&nbsp;
few hundred thousand or even a few million though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.920
He offered to invest $15 million into the company&nbsp;
in exchange for a 33% stake. Personally, I have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.160
clue why Joel decided to invest $15 million into&nbsp;
a company with no revenue. It’s also unclear why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.480
he decided to invest this money into two random&nbsp;
dudes he just met instead of just starting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:54.560
business himself. Nonetheless, that’s what he&nbsp;
decided to do and that’s how WeWork was born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.480
Over the next few years, Adam and Miguel&nbsp;
secured a bunch of high appeal locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.120
all across New York. This included a location&nbsp;
in New York’s Soho district, a location near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.280
the empire state building, and a location in&nbsp;
the meatpacking district. They would follow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:13.040
this expansion across New York with national&nbsp;
and even international expansion. In 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.640
they opened up a location in San Francisco, and&nbsp;
in 2012, they opened up a location in Tel Aviv,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.400
Israel. To Adam, it seemed like he had finally&nbsp;
hit the jackpot, but this wasn’t exactly true.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.480
A WE EXPLOSION:&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:34.000
Despite the fast expansion, both Adam and Miguel&nbsp;
were well aware that anybody with capital could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.240
just do what they were doing. At the end of&nbsp;
the day, they were just leasing commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.960
buildings breaking them up and then subleasing&nbsp;
the broken parts. So, the duo decided to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.440
elements that made them a bit unique. They&nbsp;
installed beer pubs into their buildings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.920
they put similar clients on the same floor, and&nbsp;
they started a web service called WeConnect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:56.160
allowed their clients to trade services. All&nbsp;
of these small additions gave WeWork an edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.360
over the competition, and soon enough they&nbsp;
were attracting billions of dollars. By 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:05.440
WeWork was able to get some of the most notable&nbsp;
companies in the world to invest in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.240
This included JP Morgan Chase, Goldman Sachs,&nbsp;
Harvard Corporation, Wellington Management, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.040
few others. With all this backing, WeWork was able&nbsp;
to raise hundreds of millions of dollars with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:21.360
funding round and add dozens of locations over the&nbsp;
next few years. In 2016, Fortune magazine would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.480
choose WeWork to be one of their top 3 unicorns&nbsp;
to bet on. Around the same time, SoftBank would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.280
launch their vision fund and one of their top&nbsp;
picks would of course be WeWork. Very quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.120
SoftBank would become WeWork’s biggest backer&nbsp;
pouring in $18.5 billion by the end of 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.160
It seemed like nothing could go wrong. WeWork&nbsp;
had hundreds of locations around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:47.920
and they basically had unlimited funding. But,&nbsp;
really, it wasn’t all that great. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.360
the company wasn’t even close to being profitable.&nbsp;
You see, subleasing corporate space wasn’t very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.840
lucrative as it had extremely high overhead.&nbsp;
The company was burning hundreds of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.960
every quarter, and there was still a long road to&nbsp;
profitability. So, there was no way WeWork could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.840
survive without investors. Considering this, you&nbsp;
would think that Adam would suck up to investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.160
and try his hardest to become independent&nbsp;
from them. But, it looks like Adam actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:18.160
had no problem with relying on investors. Maybe to&nbsp;
him, it seemed like an unlimited source of cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.640
but the thing he forgot about was that&nbsp;
all the investors were in it for profit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.920
And sooner or later, they’re gonna want returns.&nbsp;
Initially, though, they gave Adam a considerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.400
amount of freedom and they didn’t feel the need&nbsp;
to micromanage them. This freedom, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.600
got to Adam’s head, and it wasn’t&nbsp;
long until he was burning millions.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:39.680
A WE BUST:&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.360
One of Adam’s most notorious indulgences was&nbsp;
weed-filled flight trips. Apparently, his friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.320
and he smoked so much on board that their crew&nbsp;
had to wear oxygen masks. In one instance, Adam’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:55.760
private jet wasn’t available, so he borrowed a&nbsp;
G650ER from Gulfstream to go on a trip to Israel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.080
As you would guess, he smoked on the entire trip&nbsp;
there and he left a large amount of it inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:04.240
a cereal box to save it for the trip back. When&nbsp;
the crew found this cereal box, they weren’t very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:09.680
happy. You see, smoking onboard was one thing,&nbsp;
and transporting the substance internationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:15.040
was something else. For obvious reasons, the crew&nbsp;
nor Gulfstream wanted to be involved in this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.160
so they reported him and flew back to the US&nbsp;
without him. On another trip to Mexico City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.560
it was reported that Adam and his colleagues were&nbsp;
spitting vodka on each other and they threw up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.000
all over the cabin. And after all that, they&nbsp;
didn’t even have the decency to tip the crew.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.920
Adam quickly built up a reputation as being&nbsp;
a nightmare client amongst private pilots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.480
and flight attendants, but this negative&nbsp;
reputation wasn’t just limited to flights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.720
Adam also had a reputation for mistreating&nbsp;
employees and other executives. He didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:48.000
necessarily overwork them or abuse them per se,&nbsp;
but he had very little respect for their time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.680
For example, he often showed up hours late to&nbsp;
meetings, if he showed up at all. All of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.640
was already quite a bad image, but the final&nbsp;
straw was that WeWork wasn’t just losing money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.960
it was losing ludicrous amounts of money. In 2018,&nbsp;
for example, they brought in $1.8 billion and lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.240
$1.6 billion. At that point, they were basically&nbsp;
paying $2 for every dollar they brought it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.600
This business wasn’t any better than Adam’s&nbsp;
original baby clothing business, but this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.560
he was playing with billions instead of millions.&nbsp;
Seeing this, investors finally decided to pull the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.800
plug on WeWork and the frat boy that was running&nbsp;
it. Softbank and the rest of the board turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:30.160
on Adam and forced him to take a buyout for $500&nbsp;
million. Personally, I don’t think Adam deserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.120
anything given his terrible performance, but&nbsp;
technically this was a lot less than the $14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:40.240
billion Adam’s stake was worth at the peak. After&nbsp;
everything was said and done, Adam was left with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:46.560
somewhere between $450 million and $750 million.&nbsp;
In the meantime, his investors took losses in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:52.400
tens of billions and 2400 WeWork employees lost&nbsp;
their jobs. Usually, it’s the everyday employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:59.840
who gets screwed while investors make bank, but&nbsp;
in this case, everyone got screwed except Adam.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.080
DISGRACED BILLIONAIRE:&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.800
Given Adam’s infamous history with WeWork, it’s&nbsp;
gonna be difficult for him to start a new company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.680
and raise money, but this doesn’t really matter&nbsp;
for him given that he already has plenty of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:16.800
Even though he was publicly kicked out and&nbsp;
humiliated, he did walk away with roughly half a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.160
billion. And, with that sort of money, all he had&nbsp;
to do was make some safe investments and cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.200
back to billionaire status, and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what he did. Ever since the WeWork ousting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.080
Adam has mostly stayed out of the spotlight, but&nbsp;
recently, he did agree to do an interview after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:37.440
regaining billionaire status. In the interview,&nbsp;
he was actually given a bunch of tough questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.600
but Adam was extremely well prepared for the&nbsp;
interview and handled it really well given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.080
the circumstances. Many have gone on to call&nbsp;
Adam a genius for making a billion dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.880
while his company didn’t even make a penny.&nbsp;
Others think that Adam is a snake oil salesman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:55.840
that manipulated his way to the top. Either way&nbsp;
though, it doesn’t really look like Adam cares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.680
and I think this statement from Adam sums up&nbsp;
his thoughts pretty well. Adam has some regrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.400
but no apologies. Would you guys do what Adam&nbsp;
did if it meant becoming a billionaire? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:11.840
that down below. Also, drop a like if you wish&nbsp;
you had some of Adam’s investors by your side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:15.200
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:19.200
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

